1,2 $A “Mushrooms” – fleshy and not perennial, with gills, on many substrates$$s"Agaricales "
1,54 $B “Mushrooms” – fleshy and not perennial, with pores, on many substrates$$s"Boletaceae" "
1,62 $C “Mushrooms” – typically fleshy and not perennial, with teeth, on many substrates$$s"Hydnaceae"
1,71 $D “Mushrooms” – fleshy and funnel shaped, smooth or with ridges, the chantarelles$$s"Chantarelles"
1,85 $E Bracket fungi – woody and persistent, on trees and dead wood$$s"Polypores"
1,101 $F Puffballs, earth stars, bird's nest fungi – on soil, leaves or detritus, often tough$$s"Puffballs, earthstars, and bird's nest fungi"
1,128 $G Ascomycetes – fleshy and typically delicate$$s"Ascomycetes"
1,147 $H Coral fungi –branched or clubbed, often tough$$s"Coral fungi"
1,154 $I Jelly fungi –moist and gelatinous, becoming dry and tough, on wood$$s"Jelly fungi"
1,159 $J Stinkhorns –soft club-shaped, and smelly, on soil$$s"Stinkhorns"
1,165 $K Club shaped fungi – stinkhorns, dead mens’ fingers, earth tongues, etc.$$s"Club shaped fungi"
1,166 $L Crust fungi – dry, smooth or with pores or teeth, on wood$$s"Crust fungi"
1,175 $M Subterranean fungi$$s"subterranean fungi."
1,177 $N Slime molds$$s"Slime molds"
2,0 $_(1A) Gilled mushrooms – the Agaricales – these include classic "mushrooms" that have a cap and a stem, with the spores produced on thin "leaves" called gills, on the underside _(exception, Schizophyllum) of the cap. Some agarics, particularly those growing on wood, have an eccentric stalk, or no stalk and the cap appears to grow directly from the substrate. See the pictorial glossary for major variations in shape and the terms glossary for descriptive words. Spore colour is important for the Agaricales, and is not always related to gill colour – some coloured gills have white spores and vice versa – make a spore print! $Gilled mushrooms – the Agaricales
2,3 $a _(1A) White or light coloured spores
2,7 $b _(1A) Pink spores
2,8 $c _(1A) Dark coloured spores _(cinnamon, purple-brown, yellow-brown, grey, nearly black)
3,4 $a _(2a) Often on ground, but many substrates
3,12 $b _(2a) Often on wood, but identify by elimination Tricholomataceae,
4,5 $a _(3a) Gills free from stalk
4,6 $b _(3a) Gills attached to stalk
5,34 $a _(4a) Volva _(cup) present at base of stem, which may be fragile and also be left as warts on the cap; ring present or absent Amanitaceae,
5,35 $b _(4a) No volva of any kind, but base of stem may be bulbous; may have scales on cap, but never warts; ring usually present and often breaking loose Lepiotaceae,
6,36 $a _(4b) Gills thick and fleshy, waxy when rubbed; cap often brightly coloured; always on ground; ring usually absent Hygrophoraceae,
6,37 $b _(4b) On ground, usually near but not on wood; gills free or attached; cap often colourful and peels easily; sturdy, brittle stipe Russulaceae,
6,12 $c _(4b) Many habitats but often on wood; latex absent Tricholomataceae,
7,38 $a _(2b) Gills free from stem; usually on wood; volva present or absent Volvariaceae,
7,39 $b _(2b) Gills attached to stem; usually on ground; volva absent Rhodophyllaceae,
8,40 $a _(2c) Gills free; cap easily separable from stem Agaricaceae,
8,37 $b _(2c) On ground, usually near but not on wood; gills free or attached; cap often colourful and peels easily; sturdy, brittle stipe £Russulaceae,
8,9 $c _(2c) Gills attached
9,10 $a _(8c) Gills notably extending down stem _(decurrent), or no stem
9,11 $b _(8c) Gills attached broadly _(adnate) or narrowly _(adnexed)
18,0 $a _(14b) Gill edges serrate _(sawtooth) $Lentinus
18,0 $b _(14b) Gill edges smooth $Xeromphalina
18,0 $c _(14b) Gill edges smooth; cap covered with stiff hairs $Crinipellis
19,20 $a _(13b) Gill edges serrate or hairy
19,21 $b _(13b) Gill edges smooth
20,0 $a _(19a) Stem always absent; gills facing upwards, and split down middle $Schizophyllum
20,0 $b _(19a) Stem absent or eccentric; sawtooth gill edges $Lentinellus
21,0 $a _(19b) Cap flesh-coloured to brick red with reticulate ridges $Rhodotus
21,0 $b _(19b) Cap smooth; stem short or absent; flesh soft or tough $Pleurotus
22,23 $a _(12b) Stem with a ring
22,24 $b _(12b) Stem lacking a ring
23,0 $a _(22a) Ring always single; veil and cap appearing mealy or powdery $Cystoderma
23,0 $b _(22a) Ring single or double; veil and cap not appearing mealy $Armillaria and Catathelasma
24,25 $a _(22b) Fruiting body revives in water; stem 1-4 mm wide and pliant
24,27 $b _(22b) Fruiting body does not revive in water –or– stem usually more than 4mm wide or fleshy or brittle
25,0 $a _(24a) Cap covered with long stiff hairs $Crinipellis
25,26 $b _(24a) Cap smooth or felty
26,0 $a _(25b) Cap surface composed of round cells; small delicate mushrooms often in troops; stem yellow brown to black $Marasmius
26,0 $b _(25b) Cap cuticle composed of hyphae; small delicate mushrooms often in troops; spores turn blue in iodine $Xeromphalina
27,0 $a _(24b) Parasitic/saprotrophic on decaying mushrooms $Asterophora
27,28 $b _(24b) Not parasitic/saprotrophic on decaying mushrooms
28,0 $a _(27b) Gills adnexed to decurrent _(may be subtle) $Clitocybe/Omphalina/Omphalotus
28,29 $b _(27b) Gills adnate to adnexed but never decurrent
29,0 $a _(28b) Cap margin inrolled at first; stem brittle, sometimes stuffed; does not revive; in humus, on leaves or rotten wood; often in fairy rings $Collybia, Flammulina/Baeospora
29,30 $b _(28b) Cap margin straight or, if inrolled then stem is fleshy
30,0 $a _(29b) Relatively small and fragile; stem brittle and 1-4mm wide, equal, lacking a ring; gills broadly attached to notched, seldom decurrent; gills may be coloured, but spore print white $Mycena
30,31 $b _(29b) Stem thicker and fleshy to pliant
31,0 $a _(30b) Gills fleshy; gills or cap pink or violet $Laccaria, or Mycena pura
31,32 $b _(30b) Gills not fleshy, and usually no part pink or violet
32,0 $a _(31b) Gills bruising black, stem appears water-soaked $Lyophyllum
32,33 $b _(31b) Gills not bruising black
33,0 $a _(32b) Cap 6-45 cm broad and edge may be fluted; surface of gills and stem chalky white $Leucopaxillus
33,0 $b _(32b) Stem long and thin for size of cap; cap convex and knobbed; gills notched $Melanoleuca
33,0 $c _(32b) Stem thick, fleshy, robust; cap convex and sometimes with a depressed centre; gills broadly attached or notched $Tricholoma
34,0 $_(5a) Amanitaceae Many in this family are poisonous, often deadly, so should be avoided as potential menu items. Common features in the field include 1) white spores, 2) development from an "egg" which when cut open has stem, cap and gills _(unlike young puffballs), 3) a universal veil which either splits creating the cup _(volva) at the base of the stem and may also leave warts on the cap, 4) a partial veil that leaves a ring _(annulus) on the stem. Two genera. $Amanitaceae
34,0 $a _(5a) Volva and/or universal veil slimy and glutinous $Limacella
34,0 $b _(5a) Volva and/or universal veil fibrous or filmy or hairy $Amanita
35,0 $_(5b) Lepiotaceae This group is relatively closely related to Amanitaceae since both groups have free gills and white spores _(Chlorophyllum has green spores), but the Lepiotaceae do not have a volva even if their stem is bulbous. The cap surface is often scaly, but these are not due to remnants of the partial veil, which often forms a ring. This ring can become entirely free and slide along the stem. Many Lepiota species fruit in grass, often in fairy rings, while others appear to be mycorrhizal with conifers $Lepiotaceae
35,0 $a _(5b) Spore print and mature gills green, in grass $Chlorophyllum
35,0 $b _(5b) Spore print and mature gills white, various habitats $Lepiota
36,0 $_(6a) Hygrophoraceae This is a typically brightly coloured group _(nevertheless, the spores are white) that always grows in the ground or on duff, and not on logs or other wood, or on dung, or parasitically. When the gills are rubbed together very hard they feel waxy, and leave a waxy layer on the fingers. They do not produce latex. There is one genus, $Hygrophorus.
37,0 $_(6b) Russulaceae There are two genera in this family, both with brittle caps whose often brightly coloured cuticle peels off easily, and brittle stems that never have a ring. Both genera have white, pale brown, or yellow spores that turn blue in iodine. All species in this family grow on soil, and are typically thought to be mycorrhizal. A679-707; Ar63-103; M 62-86; $Russulaceae
37,0 $a _(6b) Produces a milky latex when cut $Lactarius
37,0 $b _(6b) Does not produce latex when cut $Russula
38,0 $_(7a) Volvariaceae One of two pink-spored families of gilled fungi. The most conspicuous characteristic of Volvariaceae is the free gills. There are two genera both of which are common _(but not exclusive) on wood $Volvariaceae
38,0 $a _(7a) Volva present at base of stem, on wood or other substrates $Volvariella
38,0 $b _(7a) Volva absent, always on wood $Pluteus
39,0 $_(7b) Rhodophyllaceae The most conspicuous feature of this pink-spored family is the attached gills. Species of this family that have central stalks are always on ground and never on wood. $Rhodophyllaceae
39,0 $a _(7b) Gills long and decurrent $Clitopilus
39,0 $b _(7b) Gills short and decurrent or notched, often with a conic cap or a knob on a convex cap $Entoloma
40,0 $_(8a) Agaricaceae Aroira and Miller recognize one genus, Agaricus, but Lincoff _(Audubon guide) includes additional genera including Lepiota. Agaricus contains a large number of edible species mushrooms form which the commercial A. bisporus was bred. Typically Agaricus are edible and choice, with the exception of A. xanthodermus and allies, which are poisonous. A. xanthodermus bruises yellow, and all Agaricus species that bruise yellowish should be avoided. The other caution is to ensure the spores are dark, since the fruiting bodies can superficially resemble Amanita. $Agaricaceae
41,0 $_(10a) Paxillaceae. Miller recognizes one genus, Paxillus, while Audubon includes three. Paxillus has clay to yellow brown spores and decurrent gills; two species grow on wood, and one on soil $Paxillaceae.
42,0 $_(10b) Gomphidiaceae Most notably, this family has thick decurrent gills and smoky-grey to black spores. There are two genera. A650-4; Ar481-7; Bo16; M164-9; $Gomphidiaceae
42,0 $a _(10b) Cap cuticle gelatinous; white flesh in cap unreactive to iodine $Gomphidius
42,0 $b _(10b) Cap dry to viscid or tacky; coloured flesh in cap turns blue in iodine $Chroogomphus
43,0 $_(11a) Coprinaceae. This family has black to purple-brown spores that are borne on attached or notched gills, which may deliquesce into inky slime. The cap is often conical with a dry to viscid cuticle, and the stem is usually relatively thin and fragile. All are saprophytes on dung, humus, grassy areas. Several are poisonous, and even the edible ones should not be eaten with alcohol. $Coprinaceae
43,0 $a _(11a) Gills white on young fruiting bodies, then black and inky with age, and/or cap with umbrella-like striations; on wood, humus, dung, grass $Coprinus
43,0 $b _(11a) Cap narrowly conic, brittle; with a thin rigid stem; single to numerous on dung or in grass, but not cespitose $Panaeolus
43,0 $c _(11a) Campanulate to convex cap, usually on humus or wood, often numerous or cespitose; many species $Psathyrella
44,0 $_(11b) Bolbitiaceae. This family is closely related to the Coprinaceae; the fruiting bodies are superficially similar, but they have lighter coloured spores. $Bolbitiaceae
44,45 $a _(11b) Stem tall and 1.2-3mm wide; cap narrowly conical and delicate
44,46 $b _(11b) Stem thicker than 3mm or if thin then cap is convex or yellow
45,0 $a _(44a) Cap white to pale cinnamon; gills nearly free $Conocybe lactea
45,0 $b _(44a) Cap yellow- to red-brown; gills nearly free $Conocybe tenera
46,0 $a _(44b) Cap yellow, bell shaped, margin striate; on cow dung in grass $Bolbitius vitellinus
46,0 $b _(44b) Cap convex, cream coloured to tan to brown, not striate $Agrocybe
47,0 $_(11c) Cortinariaceae. This is a large family with brown spores and entire gills that are always attached to the stem. Many species have the characteristic cobwebby/fibrous veil _(called a cortina) that collapses to leave a thready ring on the stem. None are found on dung, but many are saprophytic on wood, humus or soil. Many others are mycorrhizal with hardwoods or conifers. A556-8, 611-23, 712-8; Ar396-466; M202-225 $Cortinariaceae
47,48 $a _(11c) On wood, sometimes buried wood
47,50 $b _(11c) On ground, and not from buried wood
48,0 $a _(47a) Stem absent $Crepidotus
48,49 $b _(47a) Stem present, central or eccentric
49,0 $a _(48b) Fruiting body and spore print bright rusty orange to brown; cap hairless when young; stem central; from wood $Gymnopilus
49,0 $b _(48b) Spore print dull brown; cap and/or stem typically scaly or knobby; cespitose $Pholiota
50,0 $a _(47b) Veil membranous, cap orange, nearly flat, and radially wrinkled, never in cespitose clusters $Rozites caperata
50,51 $b _(47b) Veil, if present, hairy or cobwebby
51,0 $a _(50b) Stem thin, brittle, fragile, with a ring; cap thin, fragile, often striate, usually hairless, conic to convex and sometimes with a shallow depression $Galerina
51,52 $b _(50b) Stem fleshy to pliant, not brittle or fragile; cap not fragile, variously shaped, seldom striate
52,0 $a _(51b) Cap dry, conic to bell shaped, often hairy; gill edge with clavate cystidia $Inocybe
52,0 $b _(51b) Cap viscid, usually convex; veil present or absent; stem fleshy and dry; cylindric cystidia on gill edge $Hebeloma
52,0 $c _(51b) Cap dry or viscid; if viscid then no cystidia on gill edge; cobwebby veil $Cortinarius
53,0 $_(11d) Strophariaceae. This family has purple-brown spores, a central stem, and attached gills. The Agaricaceae have free gills. Continariaceae have red- to yellow-brown spores and a hairy annulus that may be a few persistent fibrils on the stem. $Strophariaceae
53,0 $a _(11d) Persistent superior ring _(except for S. aeruginosa which has almost no ring, but a viscous yellow to bright green cap) $Stropharia
53,0 $b _(11d) Thin stem, conic cap, moist-tacky-viscid; with to without ring; never cespitose; on dung, grass or humus $Psilocybe
53,0 $c _(11d) Convex cap, sometimes with low knob, occasionally with thickened stem; on wood $Naematatoloma
54,0 $_(1B). Mushrooms with pores, fleshy and not perennial – family Boletaceae. – Superficial resemblance to gilled mushrooms, but with spore-bearing surface _(hymenium) arranged on fine or coarse pores, sometimes in loose radial rows _(boletoid arrangement). $Boletaceae
54,0 $a _(1B) Cap and stalk conspicuously covered with coarse, dry black/brown scales; spores umber brown to blackish brown. Pores angular. Wooly ring_(s) on the stalk. Flesh bruising reddish, then black. $Strobilomyces
54,55 $b _(1B) If cap and stem have scales, then spores not brown/black
55,0 $a _(54b) Tubes not consistently vertical, rather contorted and may be closed. Mostly in the western regions $Gastroboletus turbinatus
55,56 $b _(54b) Tubes straight and open
56,57 $a _(55b) Tubes radially arranged in rows, with arrangement most noticeable near the stalk
56,58 $b _(55b) Tubes not radial; mouths most often round to angular, cap dry
56,0 $c _(55b) Tubes not radial; mouths most often round to angular, cap viscid $Suillus
57,0 $a _(56a) Mouths of tubes uneven; yellow; strong radial pattern resembling gills with cross-gills; 2 5mm broad, and shallow; cap brown and dry $Gyrodon merulioides
57,59 $b _(56a) Mouths of tubes even; tubes long
58,0 $a _(56b) Mouths of tubes white, then pink with age; spore print pink; bruises brown; stem with netlike ridges; taste extremely bitter $Tylopilus
58,60 $b _(56b) Mouths of tubes may be white, but not turning pink with age; not bruising brown; stalk may or may not have netlike ridges; taste mild to bitter
59,0 $a _(57b) Cap dry to moist; pores have strong radial arrangement; spores wine red to reddish brown $Fuscoboletinus
59,0 $b _(457b) Cap usually viscid to slimy; if dry then pores radially arranged; spores cinnamon or olive brown or brown $Suillus
60,0 $a _(58b) Stalk with conspicuous dark brown to black hairy tufts at maturity $Leccinum
60,61 $b _(58b) Stalk never with dark brown to black hairy tufts
61,0 $a _(60b) Spore print yellow; spores white to pale yellow; stalk not having netlike ridges $Gyroporus
61,0 $b _(60b) Cap dry; tubes fine to coarse but not radially arranged; spore print olive brown to yellow brown $Boletus
62,0 $_(1C) Fungi with spore-bearing teeth – Hydnaceae - sometimes with a appearance similar to gilled mushrooms, or like a bracket fungus – a variable group. $Hydnaceae
62,63 $a _(1C) Cap absent; fruit body covered with long, white, hanging teeth
62,66 $b _(1C) Mushroom-like appearance, with cap present; stem central, off centre, or lacking
63,64 $a _(62a) Oval fleshy mass with long spines but no branches
63,65 $b _(62a) Multiple branches with long spines
64,0 $a _(63a) White; on wounds of living hardwoods $Hericium coralloides
64,0 $b _(63a) Yellowish; on wounds of living hardwoods $Hericium weirii
65,0 $a _(63b) Teeth hanging only from branch tips $Hericium coralloides
65,0 $b _(63b) Teeth along the branch $Hericium ramosum
66,0 $a _(62b) Mushroom shaped, only on pine cones, cap small and stem off centre $Auriscalpium vulgare
66,67 $b _(62b) Never on cones; cap large; stem central or lacking
67,68 $a _(66b) Stalk present and central
67,70 $b _(66b) Stalk absent, shelf-like fungus on living trees
68,0 $a _(67a) Flesh very tough, not breaking easily $Hydnellum
68,69 $b _(67a) Flesh brittle and easily broken, with colour zones
69,0 $a _(68b) Cap dry, orange, hairless; taste mild $Dentinum
69,0 $b _(68b) Cap with scales, brown to red-brown; taste bitter $Hydnum
70,0 $a _(68b) Woody conk on conifer $Echinodontium
70,0 $b _(68b) With multiple fleshy, overlapping shelves $Steccherimun
71,0 $_(1D) Chantarelles. These basidiomycetes resemble classic mushrooms, with a cap that may be deeply depressed in the centre giving a funnel-like shape. Spores are borne on the outisde/underside of the funnel, which may be smooth or have broad gill-like ridges. $Chantarelles.
71,72 $a _(1D) Fruiting body yellow-orange-red, at least on the gills
71,82 $b _(1D) Fruiting body purple-lilac-grey-blackish
72,73 $a _(71a) Fruiting body all the same colour; cap hairless or nearly so
72,77 $b _(71a) Fruiting body of two colours, small or recurved scales on the cap
73,0 $a _(72a) Fruiting body reddish to orange $Cantharellus cinnabarius
73,74 $b _(72a) Fruiting body light orange, yellow, or creamy white
74,0 $a _(73b) Fruiting body 8-25mm wide $Cantharellus minor or Cantharellus tubaeformis
74,75 $b _(73b) Fruiting body larger
75,0 $a _(74b) Fruiting body thin with short discontinuous ridges or largely smooth, yellow to orange $Craterellus cantharellus
75,76 $b _(74b) Fruiting body fleshy with well formed gill ridges
76,0 $a _(75b) Fruiting body white with light orange tint $Cantharellus subalbidus
76,0 $b _(75b) Fruiting body orange $Cantharellus cibarius
77,78 $a _(72b) Cap funnel to trumpet shaped with large recurved scales
77,80 $b _(72b) Cap convex to somewhat depressed in centre, with small scales _(squamules)
78,0 $a _(77a) Fruiting body 3-7cm wide, fruiting in spring $Gomphus bonarii
78,79 $b _(77a) Fruiting body 5-15cm wide, fruiting in fall
79,0 $a _(78b) Spores on low blunt ridges, buff to yellow $Gomphus floccosus
79,0 $b _(78b) Spores on pore like surface, deep yellow $Gomphus kauffmanii
80,0 $a _(77b) Gills contorted and wrinkled, nearly absent, orange $Cantharellus tubaeformis, Cantharellus lutescens
80,81 $b _(77b) Gills blunt but well formed
81,0 $a _(80b) Cap dark brown, stem yellow $Cantharellus infundibuliformis
81,0 $b _(80b) Cap yellowish brown, stem greyish-orange $Cantharellus tubaeformis
82,0 $a _(71b) Never cespitose, in deep moss; cap 12-45mm wide $Cantharellus umbonatus
82,83 $b _(71b) In cespitose clusters on ground; larger caps
83,0 $a _(82b) Gills absent _(smooth hymenium); cap 20-60mm wide; very thin flesh; ashy grey to blackish $Craterellus cornucopioides
83,84 $b _(82b) Gills present, thick, fleshy, purple
84,0 $a _(83b) Deep purple, strongly warted, 1-5cm wide $Polyozellus multiplex
84,0 $b _(83b) Light purplish to purplish brown, wrinkled, 3-10 cm wide $Gomphus clavatus
85,0 $_(1E) Polypores _(Mushroom-like and bracket fungi) – tough or woody _(unlike boletes) on dead or dying trees _(although these may be underground), often perennial and some species can be very large; spore bearing surface with pores. Some polypores grow on soil or duff. $Polypores
85,0 $a _(1E) Spore bearing surface composed of free tubes $Fistulina hepatica
85,86 $b _(1E) Spore bearing surface composed of pores _(united tubes)
86,87 $a _(85b) Fruiting body forms large, woody, perennial conks, hoofshaped or shelflike; when cut in half, each layer of tubes reflects a year's growth
86,92 $b _(85b) Not hoofshaped or shelflike, or not perennial, or not with layers of tubes
87,88 $a _(86a) Upper surface smooth and shiny, shelflike fruiting body
87,89 $b _(86a) Upper surface rough, uneven, not shiny, hooflike or shelflike
88,0 $a _(87a) Upper surface grey to brown; pores bruising when scratched $Ganoderma applanatum
88,0 $b _(87a) Upper surface red to brown with white flesh $Ganoderma tsugae
88,0 $c _(87a) Upper surface orange red with light brown flesh $Ganoderma lucidum
89,90 $a _(87b) Upper surface smooth
89,91 $b _(87b) Upper surface rough and cracked
90,0 $a _(88a) Upper surface dull grey-brown to grey; hoof shaped $Fomes fomentarius
90,0 $b _(88a) Upper surface brown, shelflike or nearly flat; white pores $Fomes annosus
91,0 $a _(88b) Upper surface rough and brown, on conifers $Fomes pini or Fomes pinicola
91,0 $b _(88b) Upper surface cracked, on hardwoods $Fomes rimosus or Fomes igniarius
92,93 $a _(86b) With a stem, single or several caps, can be large
92,97 $b _(86b) Convex cap without a stem
93,0 $a _(92a) With a short lateral stem; pores angular to hexagonal $Favolus alveolaris and Polyporus squamosus
93,94 $b _(92a) Central well-developed stem with one to several caps
94,95 $a _(93a) Single cap from a central stem
94,96 $b _(93a) Multiple caps from a central stem
95,0 $a _(94a) Cap tan; stem with a black foot; on hardwood $Polyporus elegans
95,0 $b _(94a) Cap dark brown with a hairy margin, stem yellow brown, 1-2mm pores $Polyporus arcularis
95,0 $c _(94a) Cap dark brown with a smooth margin; pores <1mm $Polyporus brumalis
96,0 $a _(94b) Sulphur yellow or bright orange, hairless cap with light margin; on ground or wood $Polyporus sulphureus
96,0 $b _(94b) Multiple grey-brown caps on short white stems; white pore; on ground near wood $Polyporus frondosus
97,0 $a _(92b) Pores lilac to purplish, usually densely overlapping, on hardwood $Polyporus pargamenous
97,0 $b _(92b) Pores lilac to purplish, usually densely overlapping, on conifer logs and stumps $Polyporus abietinus
97,98 $c _(92b) Pores not purplish or pores sulphur yellow _(see Polyporus sulphureus)
98,99 $a _(97c) Pores very large _(1-3mm) and irregular, or with a gill-like arrangement
98,100 $b _(97c) Pores round, pure white or smoky grey
99,0 $a _(98a) Cap very hairy, rusty red to yellow brown $Lenzites saepiaria
99,0 $b _(98a) Cap ash grey with fine short hairs; pores large _(1-3mm) and irregular $Daedalea quercina
100,0 $a _(98b) Cap velvety with multicoloured bands; pores minute and white; in dense overlapping clusters on hardwood $Polyporus versicolor
100,0 $b _(98b) Cap hairy, yellow- to grey-brown; pores white $Polyporus hirsutus
100,0 $c _(98b) Cap velvety, smoky grey; pores grey $Polyporus adustus
101,0 $_(1F) Puffballs, earthstars, and bird's nest fungi – these basidiomycetes are distinctive fungi that typically are easy to identify to the broad group. Puffballs and earthstars are closely related in the order Lycoperdales; bird's nest fungi are in the order Nidulariales. $Puffballs, earthstars, and bird's nest fungi
101,102 $a _(1F) Puffballs
101,123 $b _(1F) Earthstars
101,127 $c _(1F) Bird's nest fungi
102,0 $_(87a) $Puffballs – rounded fruiting bodies that become dry at maturity, releasing spores through cracks or a hole _(ostiole) that develops as the puffball grows. With or without a stalk to support the fruiting bodyPuffballs
102,103 $a _(87a) Round to pear shaped, powdery surface at maturity; without a stalk _(true puffballs)
102,115 $b _(87a) Round to pear shaped on a distinctive stalk _(stalked puffballs)
102,120 $c _(87a) Resembling a puffball or an immature mushroom that never opens; may be brittle or tough at maturity but never powdery _(false puffballs)
103,0 $_(102a) True puffballs Lycoperdales, Basidiomycetes. These do not have a stem, and usually are round to pear-shaped. The outer peridium does not recurve to form rays like an earthstar. All true puffballs have powdery glebas _(spore masses) at maturity, which range from yellow to black. $True puffballs
103,104 $a _(102a) Young puffball cut in half has firm dark purplish spore mass _(gleba); skin is often thick
103,107 $b _(102a) Young puffball cut in half has firm white spore mass _(gleba); skin is usually thin
104,105 $a _(103a) Fruit body 3-6 cm across, outer skin splitting into lobes but never curving back
104,106 $b _(103a) Fruit body 4-14 cm across, outer skin splitting and curving back like an earthstar
105,0 $a _(104a) Outer skin covered with warts $Scleroderma aurantium
105,0 $b _(104a) Outer skin smooth $Scleroderma bovista
106,0 $a _(104b) Outer skin cracked and roughened $Scleroderma geaster
106,0 $b _(104b) Outer skin smooth $Scleroderma flavidum
107,108 $a _(103b) Round, oval or flattened oval; >10 cm across; if pear shaped then > 8cm across
107,109 $b _(103b) Round, < 6cm across
107,113 $c _(103b) Pear shaped or nearly so, with a sterile base; < 7cm across
108,0 $a _(107a) Puffball 10-20 cm across; surface cracked to form irregular scales $Calvatia lepidophora
108,0 $b _(107a) Puffball 20-50 cm across; surface very smooth $Calvatia gigantea
108,0 $c _(107a) Puffball 20-50 cm across; surface sculptured and cracked $Calvatia booniana
108,0 $d _(107a) Puffball 8-15 cm across; outer skin with pyramid-shaped warts $Calvatia sculpta
109,110 $a _(107b) Puffball 3-5 cm across
109,112 $b _(107b) Puffball 8-25 mm across
10,41 $a _(9a) Sessile on wood, or with stout stem and decurrent gills on wood or ground; gills forked, especially near base Paxillaceae,
10,42 $b _(9a) Gills decurrent, thick, fleshy, not turning inky; always on ground and often near conifers; never on dung Gomphidiaceae,
110,0 $a _(109a) Round, with paper thin outer skin; light and easily detatched $Bovista pila
110,111 $b _(109a) Round to oval, thicker skin, remaining attached
111,0 $a _(110b) Chalky white surface with pointed grey-tipped warts, no odour $Calvatia subcretacea
111,0 $b _(110b) smoky grey, smooth, disagreeable odour $Calvatia fumosa
112,0 $a _(109b) Chalky white with small pure white warts outer skin soon begins to crack and fall away $Lycoperdon candidum
112,0 $b _(109b) Dull white, smooth, outer wall persistent; pore in old age $Lycoperdon pusillum
113,0 $a _(107c) Outer skin light brown, smooth or with a few hairs, in clusters on wood $Lycoperdon pyriforme
113,114 $b _(107c) Outer skin with fine hairy spines, usually not on wood
