114,0 $b _(113b) Outer skin light to bark brown with 3-6mm long whitish coarse hairs $Lycoperdon echinatum
114,0 $c _(113b) Outer skin white to dingy brown with short _(1mm) separated hairs $Lycoperdon umbrinum
115,0 $_(102b) Stalked Puffballs Tulostomatales. This group is united by having a stalk which can be dry, gelatinous, or woody. Most are mycorrhizal. $Stalked Puffballs Tulostomatales
115,0 $a _(102b) Club shaped fruit body, stalk not separated from spore sac, gleba with numerous chambers $Pisolithus tinctorius
115,116 $b _(102b) Spherical to oval spore sac on a cylindrical stalk; gleba powdery and homogeneous
116,117 $a _(115b) Stalk covered with a thick gelatinous layer that may have chambers
116,118 $b _(115b) Stalk thick or woody but never gelatinous
117,0 $a _(116a) Spore sac red, at first covered with a thick red gelatinous layer _(common name is apt: the slimy stalked puffball) $Calostoma cinnabaria
117,0 $b _(116a) Spore sac yellow, stalk long, gelatinous layer very thin $Calostoma lutescens
117,0 $c _(116a) Stalk short, spore sac naked $Calostoma ravenelii
118,119 $a _(116b) Stalk >10 cm long, in desert regions
118,0 $b _(116b) Stalk 1.5-6 cm long, tough to woody, widely distributed $Tulostoma simulans
119,0 $a _(118a) Stalk 20-40 cm long, 6-15 mm wide, volva simple $Battarrea phalloides
119,0 $b _(118a) Stalk 10-45 cm long, 20-40 mm wide, volva with several layers $Battarrea laciniata
11,43 $a _(9b) Caps typically conic to bell shaped; often turning inky but when young, cap is never slimy; saprotrophs, they are often found growing on dung or manured grass or wood Coprinaceae,
11,44 $b _(9b) Similar to Coprinaceae, but never turning inky; fragile mushrooms that typically grow in grass with long thin stipes Bolbitiaceae,
11,47 $c _(9b) With a fibrous partial veil that often leaves a cobwebby ring on the stipe; never on dung; typically saprophytic on wood, humus or soil, or mycorrhizal Cortinariaceae,
11,53 $d _(9b) Sturdy fruiting bodies with a convex viscous cap; persistent annulus that is never cobwebby; usually on grass, mulch or dung Strophariaceae,
12,0 $_(3b) Tricholomataceae Any white-spored fungus that grows on wood is a member of this family, but in addition they are also found in diverse habitats and can be saprotrophic or mycorrhizal. A731-810; Ar129-237; L54-68; M87-158; Mc133; S64-91, 239 $Tricholomataceae
120,0 $_(101b) False puffballs. Outwardly similar to a puffball, but spore mass never powdery. $False puffballs
120,0 $a _(101b) Fruit body 1-5 cm wide, cross section shows only tiny chambers; rounded rubbery white to yellow peridium is stained red $Rhizopogon rubescens
120,121 $b _(101b) Gleba with branched, buff-coloured flesh, or fruiting body on a stalk
121,0 $a _(120b) Fruiting body pear shaped, greenish yellow, dry; gleba has branched, sterile buff flesh $Truncocolumella citrina
121,122 $b _(120b) Fruiting body with an obvious stem
122,0 $a _(121b) Fruiting body stalk 15-30 cm long and narrow cylindric cap 6-10cm tall; in desert $Podaxis pistillaris
122,0 $b _(121b) Stalk no more than 25 mm long; on conifer wood near snowbanks; cap yellow brown and viscid, rounded $Nivatogastrium nubigenum
122,0 $c _(121b) On earth, especially in flowerbeds and nursries; cap dingy white, hairy $Endoptychum agaricoides
123,0 $_(101b) Earthstars are a distinctive group of fungi related to puffballs, that have an outer and an inner wall. The outer wall splits at maturity _(forming rays) to expose the inner wall, but may close up under some conditions. The inner wall surrounds the spore sac _(gleba) and may develop a pore _(ostiole) at maturity. Ar699-706; M295-8 $Earthstars
123,0 $a _(101b) Rays open when wet, close around the spore sac when dry. Spore sac hairy $Astraeus hygrometricus
123,124 $b _(101b) Rays remain open, inner spore sac smooth
124,0 $a _(123b) Spore sac with many ostioles $Myriostoma coliforme
124,125 $b _(123b) Spore sac with one hole _(ostiole) at the top
125,0 $a _(124b) Inner spore sac on a short stalk $Geastrum coronatum
125,126 $b _(124b) Inner spore sac not on a stalk
126,0 $a _(125b) Spore sac in an 'bowl' when the rays open; inner surface of the bowl cracked $Geastrum triplex
126,0 $b _(125b) Spore sac exposed but not on a stalk; inner surface of the ray smooth $Geastrum saccatum
127,0 $_(101c) Bird's nest fungi are a distinctive group with tiny _(4-15 mm wide) fruiting bodies most of which remarkably resemble their common name; commonly on sticks or woody debris. There are several genera, including the most common ones described here. Ar778 81 $Bird's nest fungi
127,0 $a _(101c) Fruiting body 10-15 mm wide and high; cinnamon to grey brown; membranous covering over the top later revealing a striped inner wall; "eggs" _(peridioles) white $Cyathus striatus
127,0 $b _(101c) As for Cyathus, but inner walls not striate; peridioles nearly white $Crucibilum vulgare
127,0 $c _(101c) Fruiting body 3-10 mm wide and 5-20mm high, with a flaring mouth. "Eggs" grey to light brown, embedded in a gel. $Nidula candida
127,0 $d _(101c) Fruiting body 1.5 mm and spherical, yellow orange to whitish at maturity. The single "egg" is shot off by a ballistic mechanism given sufficient moisture $Sphaerobolus stellatus
128,0 $_(1G) Ascomycetes is a large group of typically small fungi, seldom more than 5 cm in the largest dimension, and often microscopic. Fruiting bodies are typically delicate, but may also be tough or leathery, particularly when dry. Ar782-887; M324-46 $Ascomycetes
128,129 $a _(1G) Resembling a mushroom with a 'stem' and 'cap', both of which may have an uneven _(pitted) surface, but the stem hollow and the cap never having gills, pores or teeth
128,139 $b _(1G) Resembling a cup
128,145 $c _(1G) Resembling a tongue_(s) or finger_(s) growing from soil or litter
129,0 $_(128a) Morels and false morels Morels are delicious and highly prized edible fungi, but the superficially similar false morels are generally poisonous. Typically morels resemble pine cones on stalks, fruit in springtime particularly in a forest the years first following a fire; false morels fruit spring and autumn. Ar784-816; M324-335 $Morels and false morels
129,130 $a _(128a) Head and stem continuous, hollow; head ridged or pitted _(true morel)
129,133 $b _(114a) Head or cap attached at the top of the stalk, not hollow; head wrinkled or smooth or saddled shaped, but not ridged or pitted except on the stem _(false morel)
12,13 $a _(3b) Only on wood, stem central, eccentric, or lacking
12,22 $b _(3b) On ground, needles, humus, dung, but rare on wood; stem usually central
130,0 $_(129a) True Morels A326-9; Ar784-96; M324-6 $True Morels
130,0 $a _(129a) Head light to dark brown, ridged, bell-shaped; recessed at attachment to stem; stem long and thin $Morchella hybrid and Morchella semilibera
130,131 $b _(129a) Head clearly attached directly to the stem
131,0 $a _(130b) Conical head with radial black ridges between brown pits $Morchella angustipes
131,132 $b _(130b) Ridges and pits brown or white
