164,0 $_(160b) Clathraceae Fruiting body emerging from an ‘egg’ that contains a gelatinous layer. Head often chambered or latticed. Ar772 $Clathraceae
164,0 $a _(160b) Fruiting body a stalk, with many chambers, seated in a volva _(cup) $Simblum sphaerocephalum and Clathrus columnatus
164,0 $b _(160abUpraised arms at apex, with green slime on each arm $Lysurus borealis
164,0 $c _(160b) Head regularly chambered and red $Simblum sphaerocephalum
165,0 $_(1K) Club shaped fungi – This is an artificial grouping of fruiting bodies with a similar basic morphology from a variety of groups _(stinkhorns, dead mens' fingers, jelly fungi, club fungi). Note that in this section, the intent is to distinguish between these similar groups, and then typically the key leads you to the taxonomic group, usually earlier in the key. $Club shaped fungi
165,159 $a _(1K) Erect cylindrical or club-shaped fungus, whose upper end may be tapered and covered with a smelly slime; stem relatively fragile and hollow; not branched. £Stinkhorns
165,130 $b _(1K) As in 130a, and with a small black head but without the stench Morchella
165,149 $c _(1K) Robust cylindrical or club shaped fungus, not slimy or branched Clavariadelphus
165,147 $d _(1K) Erect branched cylinders, 3-10cm tall, yellow Calocera
165,1190 $e _(1K) Tall _(10-50cm) stem with a 2-3cm knob at the tip, or a dry mushroom-like cap $_(stalked puffball) Battarrea 

